# Template for the production environment file. The real
# server/.env.production is NOT committed — the deploy workflow materializes
# it on the VM from the PROD_ENV_B64 secret stored in the GitHub
# "production" environment:
#   base64 < server/.env.production | gh secret set PROD_ENV_B64 --env production
#
# This file carries EXACTLY the one name the production stack consumes as a
# secret — the database password. The guide's server has no other secret
# env var by design: the auth topics' HS256 key is a code-level teaching
# constant (the security topic's own lesson scopes it to the demo), and
# every other GDG_* name is non-secret configuration supplied by the
# compose files themselves.
#
# SINGLE-SOURCE WIRING: the platform stack's postgres consumes
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD via interpolation
# (docker-compose-platform.yml: POSTGRES_PASSWORD=${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?…})
# and the app stack interpolates the SAME name into the server's
# GDG_DATABASE_URL (docker-compose-prod.yml). The deploy/CI shell runs
# `set -a; . server/.env.production; set +a` before any compose command, so
# ONE value feeds both sides — the database and the app cannot disagree on
# the password. POSTGRES_PASSWORD is the repo's own required-variable rail
# name, the one deploy/docker-compose.prod.yml (the deployment topic's
# artifact) already teaches.
#
# The placeholder below must pass every rail the stack enforces (compose's
# required-variable syntax rejects only an UNSET/empty value — there is no
# app-side prod-sanity secret check to satisfy) while being clearly
# non-secret. Generate the real value with:
#   openssl rand -base64 24
#
# GDG_ENV / GDG_DATABASE_URL / GDG_MONGO_URI / GDG_REDIS_ADDR stay unset in
# this FILE on purpose — docker-compose-prod.yml supplies them via its
# environment: block, which beats env_file, pointing at the platform
# datastores by network alias.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=placeholder-db-password-not-a-secret-ci-and-docs-only
